## Steps and evidence

### STEP 0 -- D0 baseline, nothing configured
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
                                            <-- empty (group 2's lists removed first)
x960-56YSQ#show platform classifier statistics utilization brief
  Loop Detection      2
  ACL                 0
  Total               2 / 1024 (0.20%)      <-- TCAM baseline
host# ping .70 from .65 -> 0% loss ; from .66 -> 0% loss
  PASS. Both sources reach the far side before any ACL exists.

### STEP 1 -- "match an IP protocol number in a NAMED hardware ACL on a port"
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44979
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny proto 1 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group t44979
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44979
      4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
                                            <-- `proto 1` CANONICALISED to `icmp`, same as the
                                                numbered range (T44987 F2)
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
   access-group t44979
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss      DENIED
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.66 ->   0% packet loss      PERMITTED
  => STEP 1 PASS: a named hardware ACL matching an IP protocol number, bound to a port with
     `access-group`, denies the matching flow and leaves an identical flow from another source
     untouched.

### STEP 2 -- FINDING: a NAMED list CAN be edited while bound (numbered lists cannot)
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44979
  % This access-list is attached to an interface. The modified configuration will be applied
    to the interface automatically after exiting this configuration mode by entering "exit"
    or "end" command.
                                            <-- ADVISORY. The prompt advanced and the edit was
                                                accepted; tools/cfg.py logged it and continued
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#1 permit proto 1 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44979
      1 permit icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      4 deny   icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  => STEP 2 PASS: the edit APPLIED to a bound list. This is the direct opposite of the
     numbered range, where the same attempt returns
     `% This access-list is currently attached to an interface so cannot be edited.` and the
     entry is silently not added (T44987 F1). The two messages differ by one word -- "is
     attached" versus "is currently attached" -- and mean opposite things.

### STEP 3 -- "first match applies"
With `1 permit` ahead of `4 deny`, both naming the identical flow:
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss        PERMITTED
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no 1
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss      DENIED again
  => STEP 3 PASS: where two entries match the same traffic with opposite actions, the lower
     sequence number wins; removing it restores the later rule. Evaluation is first-match.

### STEP 4 -- "non-matching traffic passes" -- protocol SELECTIVITY
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44979b
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny proto 6 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44979b
      4 deny tcp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32    <-- proto 6 canonicalised to `tcp`
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; no access-group t44979 ; access-group t44979b
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss        ICMP NOT denied
  => STEP 4 PASS: a rule matching protocol 6 leaves ICMP alone, on the same port, same source
     and same destination as STEP 1 where protocol 1 denied it. The protocol field genuinely
     selects. STEP 1 and STEP 4 are each other's controls.

### STEP 5 -- D4, non-IP traffic unaffected
host# ip neigh flush dev eth4 ; ping .70 from .65 (denied)
host# ip neigh show dev eth4
  10.38.22.70 lladdr 00:00:cd:42:30:29 REACHABLE
  => STEP 5 PASS: ARP re-resolved across the path while ICMP from the same host was denied.

### STEP 6 -- "repeat via service policy" (TRAP 1 observed in full)
An interface ACL is matched BEFORE any QoS policy on the same port, so the access-group must be
removed and PROVEN gone first or the QoS path is never exercised and the pass is false.
  a) with the access-group:        .65 -> 100% packet loss
  b) x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; no access-group t44979
     x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
       interface port1.0.48
                                            <-- NO access-group line
     .65 -> 0% packet loss                  <-- PROOF the ACL path is dead
  c) x960-56YSQ(config)#mls qos enable
       Traffic will stop while configuration is applied. Continue? (y/n): y
     x960-56YSQ(config)#class-map t44979cm ; match access-group t44979
     x960-56YSQ(config)#policy-map t44979pm ; class t44979cm
     x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; service-policy input t44979pm
     x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
       service-policy input t44979pm        <-- and still no access-group
     host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss     DENIED
     host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.66 ->   0% packet loss     PERMITTED
  => STEP 6 PASS: the same named hardware ACL enforces through `service-policy input` as it
     does through `access-group`, with the access-group proven removed beforehand so the QoS
     path genuinely carried the result.

